<p>
I'm preparing for a conversation with <a href="https://linkarzu.com/">linkarzu</a>.
It would be fun to quote snippets from some of the other videos he has.
I like the way he does a lot of reaction videos that build on what people have shared.
He tends to listen to things at 1.25 or 1.5x speed.
I'm not sure if I have the patience even for that,
so it'd be nice to be able to just quickly skim the text.
He also clips a lot of his own videos.
It might be interesting to play around with my workflows for listening to snippets and clipping them.
</p>

<p>
I've grabbed YouTube captions and reformatted them before:
</p>
<ul class="org-ul">
<li><a href="https://sachachua.com/blog/2021/04/grabbing-the-youtube-auto-generated-captions-is-pretty-useful-when-making-emacs-news/">Grabbing the Youtube auto-generated captions is pretty useful when making Emacs News</a> (2021)</li>
<li><a href="https://sachachua.com/blog/2021/04/org-mode-insert-youtube-video-with-separate-captions/">Org Mode: Insert YouTube video with separate captions</a> (2021)</li>
</ul>

<p>
This time around, I want to get the VTT format so that I can possibly use them with <a href="https://github.com/sachac/subed-record">subed-record</a> to clip audio or video.
</p>

<p>
From a YouTube link, I want to run a single command that gets me a VTT file with that as the media source and with the captions.
If there are any chapters in the description, it would be lovely to get them too. I can get them from the JSON, yay!
</p>

<p>
Now I can use <code>M-x sacha-subed-youtube</code> to open the VTT file, which is cached locally, and I can use <code>M-x sacha-subed-youtube-region-as-quote</code> after selecting a section. Well, actually, I have an Embark command <code>s</code> (for "subed") that I can run on a YouTube video link and a selected-mode shortcut for <code>y</code> that I can run when the region is selected. Results:
